I interviewed at a company that asked me to find bugs in their app as a live test.

I found a way to access any user's account in under 60 seconds.

The interviewer went silent.

Here's what happened:

They gave me a staging URL: "You have 1 hour. Find whatever you can."

I didn't touch the UI. I opened DevTools.

Clicked "View Profile." Watched the API call:

GET /api/users/1042

Changed it to:

GET /api/users/1043

Someone else's full profile. Name. Email. Phone. Payment history. No token check. Classic IDOR.

Then I checked password reset. The reset token was in the URL. Changed the user ID. It let me reset ANY user's password.

Account takeover. 2 API calls. No tools. Just a browser.

Total time: 47 seconds.

The interviewer: "How did you think to try that?"

Me: "I don't test if features work. I test if they can be abused."

This is what separates a QA engineer from a button-clicker.

Every API call, every URL parameter is a question:
- "What happens if I change this?"
- "What if I'm not supposed to be here?"
- "What if I send this request twice?"

Most testers ask: "Does it work?"
Security testers ask: "Does it ONLY work the way it should?"

One question finds features. The other finds vulnerabilities.

They didn't just offer me the job. They asked me to audit their entire app first.

My challenge to every QA engineer reading this:

Open your app. Find any API call with a user ID. Change the number. See what happens.

Comment SAFE if your app passes.
Comment EXPOSED if it doesn't.

Be honest. Replying to everyone.
